<h3>Three widely-assumed facts that are wrong</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>TC39 has no waiting period whatsoever.</strong> One of the most consequential standards bodies
    in software governs a language on billions of devices with zero time gate. &ldquo;Of course a serious
    process has a cooling period&rdquo; is not supported.</li>
  <li><strong>Node.js is 48 hours, not 72.</strong> The 72-hour figure that does exist applies to TSC charter
    votes, a different process.</li>
  <li><strong>Rust&rsquo;s FCP cancels rather than restarts.</strong> The widely-repeated &ldquo;resolving a
    concern restarts the 10-day countdown&rdquo; claim could not be traced to any primary source; the
    verified primary behaviour is cancellation. Cancellation is strictly stronger against the attack that
    matters &mdash; landing a placeholder, letting the clock run, and swapping in the real text at the end.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Constitutional entrenchment &mdash; the time and record devices</h2>
<p>
  The single most transferable finding: <strong>no constitutional system entrenches by duration alone.</strong>
  Denmark, the Netherlands, Belgium, Finland, Sweden, Norway and nine US states all require an
  <strong>intervening election</strong> &mdash; a change in the composition of the deciding body. Sweden&rsquo;s
  nine-month rule is measured <em>backwards from the election</em>, not forwards from the proposal; Vermont
  restricts even when a proposal may be <em>made</em> (a window every fourth year). Time is treated as
  insufficient because it does not change who is deciding.
</p>

<h3>The decisive finding for clause (i): self-declared entrenchment does not work</h3>
<p>
  In <em>Thoburn v Sunderland CC</em> [2002], Laws LJ recognised a category of &ldquo;constitutional
  statutes&rdquo; not subject to implied repeal. The load-bearing detail is <em>who assigns the
  category</em>: the court does, <strong>from outside the statute</strong>. A statute does not become immune
  by saying it is. The one case where self-referential entrenchment genuinely bound future legislatures,
  <em>AG for NSW v Trethowan</em> [1932], worked only because a <strong>higher-order instrument</strong> (the
  Colonial Laws Validity Act 1865, s.5) authorised manner-and-form entrenchment.
</p>
<p>
  Translated to a repository: an amendment that declares itself un-supersedable is decorative. The exemption
  has to be <strong>conferred by the enclosing rule</strong> &mdash; the ordinary supersede path must itself
  carry the carve-out &mdash; and enforced by something outside the amendment, i.e. a gate or a check. Roznai&rsquo;s
  <em>double amendment</em> problem is the residual: whatever confers the exemption must also protect itself,
  or it can simply be amended away first.
</p>
<p>
  Two further shapes worth recording. <strong>Germany&rsquo;s Article 79(3)</strong> and
  <strong>Brazil&rsquo;s <em>cl&aacute;usulas p&eacute;treas</em></strong> are textual eternity clauses &mdash;
  Brazil&rsquo;s bars even <em>deliberation</em>, which is stronger than barring adoption &mdash; and both are
  enforced by a constitutional court, again from outside. <strong>Finland&rsquo;s &sect;73</strong> takes the
  opposite tack and <em>prices</em> the override rather than barring it: the waiting period may be skipped by a
  5/6 vote where 2/3 suffices to pass. That is the cleanest known answer to the double-amendment hole, because
  the override is explicit and expensive rather than pretended away &mdash; but pricing needs a headcount to
  price with.
</p>

<h2>Does a cooling period actually do anything? The honest answer</h2>
<p>
  The empirical record is weak to negative, and the survey reports it rather than burying it:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Utah&rsquo;s 72-hour waiting period</strong> (ANSIRH): only ~2% of respondents who arrived
    certain later decided against. Those who changed their minds were mostly still deciding at the first
    visit. A mandatory delay overwhelmingly <em>postpones the same decision</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Anchoring is not debiased by delay.</strong> Studies inserting a time gap between anchor
    exposure and estimate found no significant difference across delay conditions.</li>
  <li><strong>The unconscious-thought advantage failed replication</strong> (Nieuwenstein et al. 2015, n=399,
    ~10&times; prior studies). Do not cite it.</li>
  <li><strong>The one solid positive</strong> is Bu&ccedil;inca, Malaya &amp; Gajos (CSCW 2021): cognitive
    forcing functions &mdash; including a forced wait &mdash; significantly reduced over-reliance on AI. The
    effective wait was <strong>30 seconds</strong>. The mechanism is interrupting the automatic accept, not
    elapsed time. The authors also report the cost: people over-relied less exactly under the conditions they
    liked and trusted least.</li>
  <li><strong>Wagner et al. (Nature, 2004)</strong> remains the cleanest evidence for a discontinuity at
    <em>one night&rsquo;s sleep</em> &mdash; more than twice the insight rate &mdash; but on lab rule-discovery,
    not on reversing a considered decision.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  So the defensible claim for clause (ii) is <strong>not</strong> &ldquo;days of reflection produce a better
  judgment.&rdquo; It is that the interval creates a <strong>window in which new information can arrive</strong>,
  and &mdash; paired with a structural condition &mdash; guarantees the ratifying act is performed from a
  different state than the authoring one.
</p>

<h2>Durable-record conventions</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>ADR (Nygard, 2011)</strong>: Title / Status / Context / Decision / Consequences, and explicitly
    &ldquo;if a decision is reversed, we will keep the old one around, but mark it as superseded.&rdquo;
    <code>adr-tools</code> writes the pointer in <em>both</em> directions.</li>
  <li><strong>MADR 4.0.0</strong> encodes it in frontmatter: <code>status: … superseded by ADR-0123</code>.
    <strong>PEP 1</strong> uses paired <code>Replaces:</code> / <code>Superseded-By:</code> headers.</li>
  <li><strong>IETF</strong>: a published RFC is never edited; supersession lives entirely in the <em>new</em>
    document&rsquo;s <code>Obsoletes:</code> header, with the forward pointer materialised by the index.</li>
  <li><strong>OpenAI&rsquo;s Model Spec</strong> keeps a real changelog plus a dated archive of every released
    version. <strong>Anthropic&rsquo;s usage policy</strong>, by contrast, exposes an effective date and a
    single &ldquo;Previous Version&rdquo; link &mdash; lineage depth one, no diff. If you were going to cite
    &ldquo;Anthropic does it this way&rdquo; as precedent for a record format, the precedent points the other
    way.</li>
  <li><strong>Anthropic&rsquo;s red-team work</strong> published 38,961 attack transcripts as a separate
    linked dataset rather than inlining summaries &mdash; the precedent for treating a transcript as its own
    artifact beside the decision.</li>
  <li><strong>Counter-signal:</strong> bidirectional supersede pointers are <em>not</em> settled. Ethereum
    removed its <code>superseded-by</code> header and closed the proposal to restore it as not planned;
    Kubernetes&rsquo; KEP metadata carries only a backward <code>replaces:</code> with no forward pointer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What this repo already has, and what it lacks</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No cooling period anywhere.</strong> <code>preparedDate</code> and <code>dateResolved</code> are
    both written and <em>never compared</em>; readiness reads &ldquo;prepared&rdquo; as a boolean. The only
    calendar arithmetic in the repo is research freshness (<code>addIsoDuration</code>), which is the
    primitive to reuse.</li>
  <li><strong>No supersede mechanism to be exempt from.</strong> #911&rsquo;s supersede-with-lineage is
    documented prose only &mdash; no CLI verb, no frontmatter field, no validator. The one genuinely
    mechanized supersede-with-lineage in the repo is the <em>research-topic</em> chain, whose entrenchment
    property comes from the immutability of the dated artifact rather than from a permission check.</li>
  <li><strong>One whole-file statute gate.</strong> Every diff to the statute file routes to the same human
    gate; nothing distinguishes a constitution-tier anchor from an ordinary one. That identification
    mechanism is what
    <a href="/backlog/2568-constitution-curation-form-which-core-principles-vs-specs-an/">#2568</a>&rsquo;s
    Fork 1 produces.</li>
  <li><strong>Actor identity exists and is honest about its limits.</strong> The review-independence module
    compares the PR author&rsquo;s harness session id against the clearer&rsquo;s, and says plainly in its own
    docblock that the id is forgeable by an agent with shell access &mdash; what it buys is that self-clearing
    takes a deliberate forge and leaves a named record.</li>
  <li><strong>The jury ledger cannot serve as a committed record</strong> &mdash; it writes to gitignored
    operational state. The <code>reports/</code> channel is the only committed, gate-validated artifact home:
    an orphaned report is a hard gate error, and an authored-but-uncommitted one is flagged before push.</li>
  <li><strong>A per-project constitution already exists in the wild</strong> &mdash; plateau-app publishes one
    at a public route, rendered from the repo-root file so it cannot drift. It is a <em>product north-star</em>
    that explicitly disclaims build decisions, and #2564&rsquo;s &ldquo;project&rdquo; means a <em>WE standards
    project</em> (the owner of intents and protocols), not a repository &mdash; so the overlap is a vocabulary
    collision, and it is exactly why tier membership should turn on a declared index rather than a filename.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What the evidence supports</h2>
<p>
  <strong>On the day-count.</strong> Four defensible candidates, each with its strongest precedent: 3 days
  (Apache&rsquo;s floor, but its rationale is timezone coverage of a voting body and degenerates solo);
  <strong>10 days</strong> (Rust &mdash; the only derivation that transfers, &ldquo;ten calendar days so that
  it is open for at least 5 business days&rdquo;); 14 days (the modal, convergent answer &mdash; but no source
  says why); 28 days (W3C&rsquo;s minimum, and IETF&rsquo;s doubling for submissions with <em>no prior
  deliberating body</em>, which is structurally always the solo case). The honest headline is that
  <strong>no source derived its number from evidence</strong> &mdash; every stated rationale is operational or
  rhetorical &mdash; so the number should be the one whose derivation can be restated in a sentence, and it
  should be <em>paired with a structural condition</em>, because every constitutional system refuses to rely on
  time alone.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>On the record format.</strong> A per-amendment dated file for the record (the ADR/MADR/PEP
  convention), with the red-team transcript as a committed sibling artifact rather than a pull-request thread
  &mdash; comments are editable and deletable, commits are not. One deliberate divergence from the ADR
  convention follows from clause (i): an amendment record carries no <code>superseded-by</code> field, because
  removing the supersede path <em>is</em> the entrenchment. That makes the IETF immutable-document model the
  closer analogue.
</p>

